  <slide>
    <title>In class: the skeletal notes</title>
    <p>The <term>hyperbolic cosine</term> and <term>hyperbolic sine</term> are defined as
      <md>\cosh x = \frac{e^x + e^{-x}}{2}, \qquad \sinh x = \frac{e^x - e^{-x}}{2}.</md>
    </p>
    <p>Find the derivatives <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}(\cosh x)</m> and <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}(\sinh x)</m> by differentiating the two definitions.</p>
    <image source="images/workspace.svg" width="92%"><shortdescription>Blank workspace for a handwritten derivation.</shortdescription></image>
    <p><alert>Checkpoint.</alert> True or False: the parametrization <m>x = -\cosh t</m>, <m>y = -\sinh t</m>, <m>t \in \R</m>, also traces the left branch of <m>x^2 - y^2 = 1</m>. Your answer and reasoning.</p>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>After class: the completed notes</title>
    <p>The <term>hyperbolic cosine</term> and <term>hyperbolic sine</term> are defined as
      <md>\cosh x = \frac{e^x + e^{-x}}{2}, \qquad \sinh x = \frac{e^x - e^{-x}}{2}.</md>
    </p>
    <p>The derivatives are
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}(\cosh x) \amp = \sinh x</mrow>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}(\sinh x) \amp = \cosh x</mrow>
      </md>
    Both follow directly from the definitions, since <m>\frac{d}{dx}e^x = e^x</m> and <m>\frac{d}{dx}e^{-x} = -e^{-x}</m>.</p>
    <p><alert>Checkpoint.</alert> In the web book the same checkpoint is an interactive True/False question with the book's explanation.</p>
  </slide>

